Team,

I respectfully ask the team to consider the recommendation below for a replacement system that supports PTP, rather than only NTP. The context is the replacement of the ESE ES-185F GPS master clock. The replacement solution must continue supporting our Meinberg TCR180PEX IRIG card and remain within an approximate budget range of $3,000 to $6,000.

Why PTP rather than only NTP

Sub-microsecond, hardware-timestamped time is a critical requirement for star-tracker fusion, given the 50 µrad LOS budget. A 1 ms NTP skew translates to approximately 7 meters of position error at engagement range. It is also important for MCAP cross-host replay correlation and for maintaining the SIL-3 evidence chain. PTP timestamps are deterministic and auditable, while NTP timestamps are not. All five candidates listed below also provide NTP services, so the utility plane remains covered in either case.

Ranked candidates for our use case

1. Meinberg microSync RX HR - Recommended
    This is the preferred option due to vendor alignment with the TCR180PEX, which helps preserve a single timing chain and identical signal-quality semantics. Multi-GNSS support is standard, it supports all major PTP profiles, and it provides the strongest management interface of the candidates reviewed. A typical configuration is in the $5,000 to $7,000 range.
2. Microchip SyncServer S650
    This option provides the best raw PTP precision, at up to ≤15 ns to UTC, along with the strongest holdover performance, with the rubidium option rated at <1 µs over 24 hours. It is the strongest technical option if the SIL-3 hazard case requires rubidium-class holdover. However, it introduces a different vendor into the timing chain and pushes the upper end of the budget range.
3. Meinberg LANTIME M200/M300 with PTP card
    This is a solid same-vendor alternative. It is a mature platform, though older, and the entry-tier configuration is GPS-only. It should be considered a fallback option if the microSync RFQ exceeds budget.
4. Safran SecureSync 1200
    This is the only candidate in the set that offers a MIL-STD-810 ruggedized chassis and a SAASM / HaveQuick path. It should be considered only if the program has a firm requirement for military ruggedization or anti-spoof GPS capability.
5. EndRun Tempus LX
    This is the lowest-cost option among the five. However, it comes from a smaller vendor and provides a less detailed IRIG-B specification. It should be bench-validated before being relied upon in this application. It is best suited for cases where cost is the primary driver.

